
# Throttlr ⚡
### Distributed Rate Limiting Service

Throttlr is a distributed rate-limiting service designed to control API request traffic across multiple application instances. Built with **FastAPI and Redis**, the project explores atomic rate-limit enforcement, distributed state management, concurrency, observability, and performance benchmarking.

The goal is to build a reliable, scalable, and measurable rate-limiting system while exploring practical distributed-systems concepts.

> **Project status:** In development. Features listed in the roadmap are planned and will be marked complete as they are implemented and tested.

## 🎯 Objectives

- Implement configurable rate-limiting algorithms.
- Enforce shared request quotas across multiple API instances.
- Prevent race conditions using atomic Redis operations and Lua scripts.
- Support API-key-based identification and configurable policies.
- Monitor request throughput, rejection rates, and latency.
- Benchmark performance under concurrent workloads.
- Provide a reproducible, containerized development environment.

## 🛠️ Tech Stack

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Python | Core implementation |
| FastAPI | REST API and request validation |
| Redis | Shared, low-latency rate-limit state |
| Redis Lua scripts | Atomic rate-limit decisions |
| PostgreSQL | Persistent policies and API-key metadata |
| SQLAlchemy | Database access and ORM |
| Alembic | Database schema migrations |
| Docker & Docker Compose | Containerization and local orchestration |
| Prometheus | Metrics collection |
| Grafana | Monitoring dashboards |
| pytest | Unit, integration, and concurrency testing |
| Locust | Load and performance testing |
| GitHub Actions | Continuous integration |

## 🏗️ Architecture

```text
                  Client Applications
                          |
                          v
                 Load Balancer / Proxy
                          |
             +------------+------------+
             |            |            |
             v            v            v
         FastAPI A    FastAPI B    FastAPI C
             |            |            |
             +------------+------------+
                          |
                          v
                 +----------------+
                 |      Redis     |
                 |                |
                 | Atomic Scripts |
                 | Counters       |
                 | Token State    |
                 +----------------+
                          |
                  Rate-Limit Decision

       +----------------+----------------+
       |                                 |
       v                                 v
   PostgreSQL                      Observability
   Policies                        Prometheus
   API-Key Metadata                Grafana
```

### How it works

1. A client sends an HTTP request with its API key.
2. The API identifies the client and resolves the applicable rate-limit policy.
3. The selected algorithm evaluates the request using shared Redis state.
4. Redis performs the relevant state update and decision atomically.
5. The service returns an allow or deny decision with quota information.
6. Metrics capture request outcomes and system performance.

All application instances share Redis state so that distributing requests across instances does not independently reset each instance's quota.

## ⚙️ Rate-Limiting Algorithms

The algorithms will be implemented incrementally.

| Algorithm | Description | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Fixed Window | Limits requests within fixed time intervals | Planned |
| Token Bucket | Supports controlled bursts and token refill | Planned |
| Sliding Window Log | Tracks requests over a rolling time window | Planned |
| Sliding Window Counter | Approximates rolling limits with lower storage overhead | Planned |

Each algorithm will use a common interface so that the service can support different policies without coupling algorithm logic to HTTP endpoints.

## ✨ Planned Features

- Distributed rate-limit enforcement.
- Configurable request limits and time windows.
- Per-client and per-route policies.
- API-key authentication.
- Atomic Redis operations for concurrency safety.
- HTTP `429 Too Many Requests` responses.
- `Retry-After` and rate-limit response headers.
- Redis failure handling with configurable fail-open or fail-closed behavior.
- Prometheus metrics and Grafana dashboards.
- Concurrent load testing and benchmark reports.
- Automated testing and continuous integration.

